              "asynchronous_ai_overview": false,
              "markdown": "Hebrew is read and written **from right to left**.[[1]](https://hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Introduction/Basic_Features/basic_features.html)\n\n### Key Rules of Reading Hebrew\n\n- **Right-to-Left Direction:** You start reading a line on the far right and move toward the left. Books also open from what English speakers consider the \"back\" (the right side) and flip toward the left.[[1]](https://hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Introduction/Basic_Features/basic_features.html)\n- **Consonant Alphabet:** The [Hebrew Alphabet](https://www.myjewishlearning.com/article/how-to-read-the-hebrew-alphabet/) (*Alef-Bet* ) has 22 letters, all of which are consonants . There are no capital letters.[[1]](https://mena-languages.northwestern.edu/language-learning/languages-with-different-scripts/hebrew-alphabet.html)[[2]](https://hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Introduction/Basic_Features/basic_features.html)\n- **Vowels and Nikkud:** Standard modern Hebrew texts are written without vowel letters. Instead, native readers rely on context and word patterns. For beginners, children's books, poetry, and religious texts, a system of dots and dashes called **nikkud** (*niqqud* ) is placed above, below, or inside the letters to indicate vowel sounds.[[1]](https://hebrewglot.com/en/blog/how-to-read-hebrew)[[2]](https://mena-languages.northwestern.edu/language-learning/languages-with-different-scripts/hebrew-alphabet.html)[[3]](https://moderntalkbooks.com/how-to-read-hebrew/)\n- **Final Forms (*Sofit*):** Five letters (*Kaf, Mem, Nun, Peh,* and *Tzade* ) change their shape and are written differently when they appear at the very end of a word.[[1]](https://mena-languages.northwestern.edu/language-learning/languages-with-different-scripts/hebrew-alphabet.html)\n- **Dual-Sound Letters:** A few letters can make two different sounds depending on whether they contain a dot called a *dagesh* inside them (for example, *Bet* can sound like \"B\" with a dot or \"V\" without it).[[1]](https://www.myjewishlearning.com/article/how-to-read-the-hebrew-alphabet/)\n\nWould you like to learn the first few letters of the **Alef-Bet** or practice sounding out a simple **Hebrew word**?",
