                  "text": "Right-to-Left Direction: You start reading a line on the far right and move toward the left. Books also open from what English speakers consider the \"back\" (the right side) and flip toward the left. Consonant Alphabet: The Hebrew Alphabet (Alef-Bet) has 22 letters, all of which are consonants. There are no capital letters. Vowels and Nikkud: Standard modern Hebrew texts are written without vowel letters. Instead, native readers rely on context and word patterns. For beginners, children's books, poetry, and religious texts, a system of dots and dashes called nikkud (niqqud) is placed above, below, or inside the letters to indicate vowel sounds. Final Forms (Sofit): Five letters (Kaf, Mem, Nun, Peh, and Tzade) change their shape and are written differently when they appear at the very end of a word. Dual-Sound Letters: A few letters can make two different sounds depending on whether they contain a dot called a dagesh inside them (for example, Bet can sound like \"B\" with a dot or \"V\" without it).",
